    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This project examines the underlying mechanisms behind differential offspring size in two closely related species of sympatric freshwater swordtails that are endemic to rivers in Mexico and Central America. Some recently diverged species of the Xiphophorus genus of swordtails differ significantly in size from each other, both as newborns (or “fry”) and as adults. Intriguingly, the species that are larger in adulthood produce smaller newborn offspring. To explore this phenomenon, embryo development and early-life growth rate were tracked to better understand differential size of both embryos and fry. This work makes clear that the two sister species have different levels of lecithotrophy–a reproductive strategy that determines how much maternal resource provisioning occurs over the course of gestation. Additionally, fat content and early-life growth rate are assessed to determine energy surplus and metabolic rate, ultimately showing that resource abundance may be linked to higher growth and metabolism in these fish. While this research established some understanding of the underlying mechanisms of differential offspring size, there is still a gap in understanding what ecological pressures may be driving the smaller adult fish to produce larger offspring. To begin addressing this, early experimentation was completed to begin understanding how diet can be compared between these two species, so that the groundwork is laid for successful gut metabarcoding and identification of present taxa in each fish’s diet. This work will hopefully provide insights into differential resource availability for each of these species, which may be an important consideration as these species’ habitats change or shrink under the current trajectories of anthropogenic impact.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
